# SR&ED WIP file (mandatory on feature branches)

When **substantive** work is done on a git branch whose name starts with `feature/` (code, tests, API, pipeline logic — not pure copy/formatting), the agent must **not** rely on chat as the log.

## Branch slug and path

- Run `git branch --show-current`; replace every `/` with `-` → **branch slug**.
- WIP path: `docs/sred/wip-<branch-slug>.md`
- If the file is missing, **create it** with a top-level `## WIP entries` section (and a one-line title if helpful).

## When to append

Before considering the task complete for that turn, **append** at least one bullet under `## WIP entries` when any of these occurred in this session (same criteria as `.cursorrules`):

- Technological uncertainty, hypothesis, rejected approach, measurement/test outcome, or non-obvious trade-off.

For **routine** work that matches auto-disqualify in `.cursorrules`, append a single line: `- Routine: <one phrase> (no SRED thread).`

**UI / styling-only** changes: at most one routine line as above; no long narrative.

**Honesty:** Do not invent experiments. If nothing qualified was recorded during the work, write `Not recorded in WIP` once rather than backfilling.

## Do not

- Put secrets, credentials, or personal data in the WIP file.

## Full policy

The complete workflow (commit message, SRED TECHNICAL LOG, archiving) lives in `.cursorrules` under "SR&ED contemporaneous notes & commit workflow".
